The two directions, verified:

```js
// SPREAD — one collection → many elements
const arr1 = [1, 2];
const withMore = [...arr1, 3];            // [1,2,3] ✓
const mergedObj = { ...{a: 1}, b: 2 };    // {a:1, b:2} ✓
console.log(Math.max(...[3, 1, 2]));       // 3 — array → args ✓
const cloned = [...arr1];                   // new array (shallow) ✓

// REST — many elements → one collection
function sum(...items) {                   // gathers ALL args
  return items.reduce((s, n) => s + n, 0); // items is a REAL array
}
sum(1, 2, 3, 4);                           // 10 ✓
const [head, ...tail] = [1, 2, 3, 4];     // head=1, tail=[2,3,4]

// The distinction in one line each:
// f(...arr)  — spread: I HAVE an array, you WANT separate args
// f(...rest) — rest:   you SEND separate args, I WANT an array
```

The replacement uses (what spread eliminated from older code):
- Copy: `[...arr]` replaced `arr.slice()` / `arr.concat()`
- Merge objects: `{...a, ...b}` replaced `Object.assign({}, a, b)` — note right-wins on key conflicts (b's values overwrite a's), the behavior you rely on for overrides: `{...defaults, ...userConfig}`
- Array-to-args: `Math.max(...nums)` replaced `Math.max.apply(null, nums)`

The rest-vs-arguments modernization (the follow-up): `function f() { arguments }` gives an array-LIKE object — indexed and has length, but NO map/filter/reduce, and it's absent in arrow functions. Rest params are a real array, work in arrows, and can be positioned after named params: `function f(prefix, ...rest)`.
